# Buddhism and Jainism: The Shramana Challenge

> In the sixth century BCE, two Kshatriya teachers — Siddhartha Gautama and
> Vardhamana Mahavira — rejected Vedic ritual and varna hierarchy to preach
> in the people's languages. Their orders reshaped Indian religion, ethics,
> and art for the next two millennia.

## 1. The Sixth-Century Ferment

By 600 BCE the Gangetic plains had iron ploughs, punch-marked silver coins,
new cities — and religious restlessness: Buddhist texts count **62 sects**.
The causes behind the great **shramana** ("striver") challenge:

| Cause | Explanation |
| --- | --- |
| Ritualism | Costly yajnas had become priestly business |
| Varna inequality | Brahmanical supremacy resented, especially by Kshatriyas |
| New economy | Trade and agriculture favoured a non-violent, honest ethic |
| Urban moneyed class | Vaishyas sought status outside the varna ladder |
| Language | Sanskrit was closed; Pali and Prakrit opened the door |

Both founders were **Kshatriyas**, and both taught in the speech of the
people — a direct answer to the ritual class.

## 2. The Life of the Buddha

Siddhartha Gautama (traditionally **563-483 BCE**) was born at **Lumbini**
(Nepal; marked by an Ashokan pillar) in the **Shakya** republic, son of
chief **Suddhodana** and queen **Mahamaya**. Leaving wife Yashodhara and
son Rahula at 29 — the **mahabhinishkramana** — he found austerity barren
and under a **peepal tree** at Uruvela on the river **Niranjana** attained
enlightenment at 35: **Bodh Gaya**.

@diagram tree id="buddha-life-places" layout="tree" aria="Four key places in the Buddha's life"
  Buddha's Life -> "Lumbini: birth"
  Buddha's Life -> "Bodh Gaya: enlightenment"
  Buddha's Life -> "Sarnath: first sermon"
  Buddha's Life -> "Kushinagar: mahaparinirvana"
@/diagram

He preached his first sermon at the deer park of **Sarnath** — the
**dharmachakrapravartana**, "turning of the wheel of law" — and died at 80
at **Kushinagar**, the **mahaparinirvana**, his relics divided into eight
shares for the first stupas.

## 3. The Dhamma: Core Doctrine

Buddhism centres on the **Four Noble Truths**: life is **dukkha**
(suffering); suffering arises from **trishna** (craving); it can cease;
and the way out is the **Noble Eightfold Path** — right view, resolve,
speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness, and concentration — the
**Middle Way** between luxury and self-torture. The Buddha taught
**anatmavada** (no permanent soul) yet accepted karma and rebirth; the
goal is **nirvana**, the blowing-out of craving. The **Triratna** are the
Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha.

[important title="No god, no soul — yet ethics"]
The Buddha stayed silent on a creator and denied a permanent self, but
insisted on an iron moral law: karma. The Five Precepts for laity —
non-violence, truth, no stealing, no sexual misconduct, no intoxicants —
made the path practical.
[/important]

## 4. The Sangha and the Canon

The **Sangha** admitted all castes — and, at Ananda's urging through
Prajapati Gautami, women as **bhikkhunis**. Monks lived by the Vinaya
rules recited at **uposatha**, the fortnightly assembly. The Pali canon,
the **Tipitaka**, has three baskets: **Vinaya** (discipline), **Sutta**
(sermons), **Abhidhamma** (philosophy).

## 5. Buddhist Councils and Sects

| Council | Place | Year | Patron / President | Outcome |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| First | Rajgriha | 483 BCE | Ajatashatru / Mahakassapa | Sutta and Vinaya compiled |
| Second | Vaishali | 383 BCE | Kalasoka / Sabakami | Discipline dispute; first split |
| Third | Pataliputra | 250 BCE | Ashoka / Moggaliputta Tissa | Abhidhamma added; missions sent |
| Fourth | Kashmir | 72 CE | Kanishka / Vasumitra | Hinayana-Mahayana division formalised |

**Mahayana** ("great vehicle") made the Buddha a saviour god served by
**Bodhisattvas** in Sanskrit; **Hinayana**/Theravada kept individual effort
and the human master; **Vajrayana** added tantric method and flourished
under the Palas in Bengal and Tibet.

## 6. Mahavira and the Jain Path

Vardhamana Mahavira (**540-468 BCE**), the **24th Tirthankara**, was born at
**Kundagrama** near Vaishali — father Siddhartha, mother Trishala. He
renounced home at 30 and after twelve years gained **kaivalya**
(omniscience) under a sal tree at Jrimbhikagrama, dying at 72 by voluntary
fast at **Pavapuri**. The line of Tirthankaras begins with
**Rishabhanatha (Adinatha)**; the 23rd, **Parshvanatha**, already taught
four vows. Mahavira's five great vows (**vrata**):

| Vow | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Ahimsa | Non-violence toward all life |
| Satya | Truth |
| Asteya | Not taking what is not given |
| Aparigraha | Non-possession |
| Brahmacharya | Celibacy — Mahavira's addition |

[definition]
**Anekantavada** — the doctrine of "many-sidedness": truth is too vast for
one viewpoint. Its logical arm, **syadvada**, judges every statement true
only "in some respect" — an epistemology of humility.
[/definition]

## 7. Jain Councils and the Great Split

| Council | Place | Time | Outcome |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| First | Pataliputra | c. 300 BCE | Sthulabhadra led; 12 Angas compiled after famine |
| Second | Vallabhi | 512 CE | Devardhigani wrote the Agamas in Ardha-Magadhi |

After the famine-era migration south with **Bhadrabahu** — Chandragupta
Maurya, by tradition, went with him — the community split: **Digambara**
("sky-clad," monks naked, scriptures lost) and **Svetambara** ("white-clad,"
Agamas preserved). Jainism never left India, but won lasting patrons —
Udayin, Chandragupta Maurya, Kharavela of Kalinga, and the Rashtrakutas
and Chandelas.

## 8. Buddhism and Jainism Compared

| Aspect | Buddhism | Jainism |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Path | Middle Way | Strict austerity, even sallekhana |
| Soul | No permanent self (anatmavada) | Soul exists in all things |
| Ahimsa | Moderate | Absolute — monks sweep their path |
| Language | Pali | Prakrit (Ardha-Magadhi) |
| Spread | Sri Lanka, SE and East Asia | Stayed within India |
| Patrons | Bimbisara, Ajatashatru, Ashoka, Kanishka | Udayin, Chandragupta, Kharavela |

[note]
Buddhism faded in the land of its birth — the Turkish destruction of
Nalanda and Vikramashila (c. 1193-1203) is the symbolic end — while
Jainism survived through merchant communities of Gujarat and Rajasthan.
[/note]

## Key Takeaways

- Both faiths answered the same sixth-century crisis: ritual cost, varna
  pride, and a trading economy needing an ethic of restraint.
- The Buddha's core is the Four Noble Truths and the Middle Way; the Jain
  core is the five vows and anekantavada.
- Four councils (Rajgriha to Kashmir) shaped Buddhist scripture and its
  Hinayana-Mahayana-Vajrayana division; Jain councils at Pataliputra and
  Vallabhi preserved the Agamas.
- Jainism's absolutism kept it Indian; Buddhism's flexibility carried it
  across Asia — and eventually out of India.
